# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetPropertyCalls<T>` → `SetPropertyBuilder<T>`

The type was **renamed**. Your manual expression-tree code referencing `SetPropertyCalls<Blog>` won't compile.

**Fix:**

```csharp
Expression<Func<SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>, SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>>> setters =
    s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
if (nameChanged)
{
    var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
    setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>, SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>>>(
        Expression.Call(
            instance: setters.Body,
            methodName: nameof(SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>.SetProperty),
            typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
            arguments: new Expression[]
            {
                Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                    Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
                Expression.Constant("foo")
            }),
        setters.Parameters);
}
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(setters);
```

---

## 2. Complex Type Column Naming Convention Changed

EF Core 10 now prefixes complex-type columns with the navigation name (e.g., `ShippingAddress_Street`, `BillingAddress_Street`). This **breaks existing database schemas** silently—migrations will try to rename columns.

**Fix** — pin the old convention with explicit column mapping:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street");
        // ... other properties keep old names
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
        // keep whatever your existing schema uses
    });
});

// For nested: use chained ComplexProperty calls
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb => {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab => {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

Or, if you want the **new convention** everywhere, just generate a migration and apply it.

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — New `CommandSource` Parameter

The interface methods now include a `CommandSource` parameter.

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new required parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

## Summary

| Issue | Change | Action |
|-------|--------|--------|
| Dynamic `ExecuteUpdate` expressions | `SetPropertyCalls<T>` → `SetPropertyBuilder<T>` | Rename the type everywhere |
| Complex type columns | Default names now include nav prefix | Add explicit `HasColumnName()` or migrate DB |
| Diagnostics logger | `CommandSource` param added | Add parameter to implementation |
